  "notes": "⛔ **One parameter carries this whole model, and it is the inverse of what you would expect.** The research: \"the beat stays **strictly quantised precisely because the vocal does not**\". Silkk's signature is phrasing that floats off the grid — and the instrumental answer to that is a machine-tight backing track, so `humanize.quantizeStrength` goes to 0.98 and every `timingJitterMs` lane falls to 0 or 1. That makes this the tightest model on this axis by a wide margin, against a parent sitting at 0.86 and `big-tymers` at 0.81. It is deliberately the opposite of a \"human feel\" setting: the human feel was in the voice, and the engine has no voice. ⚠ **The off-grid vocal offset itself is not modelled and cannot be.** The research asks for \"vocal phrase start offsets of roughly ±1/16 to ±1/8 against the grid\", marks it [LOW-CONF, characterisation of an audible trait rather than a sourced claim], and there is no vocal lane to put it on. Displacing the melody instead would be a lie about the instrumental, which the research says is quantised. It is recorded here rather than faked. ⚠ **Everything else is the Beats By the Pound bench:** minor vamps, `i–VII–VI` at 4 bars, 808/909 kick, snare on 2 and 4, continuous 16th hats, and a synth bass with 808 sub reinforcement — so `offSnare.prob` is low (0.34, the bounce answer is not the default here), `hihat` is a continuous 16th stream, and the low end stays two-voice. `melody.phraseBars` is weighted to 4 because the research names 4-bar loops, with 2 kept live so the derived parts are not bounded by one length. ⛔ **Confidence is `low`, and that is the research's verdict** — \"medium-low — era/label/bench sourced; the off-grid phrasing parameter is inference\". The 82–96 band is marked **[LOW-CONF]** on top of that. Inferred: the key list, every velocity and register bound, `intervals`/`contour`, the ghost-snare positions, the perc lanes and the chord-duration spread. ⚠ `snareRoll.pitchWalk` is inherited.",
